data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` with native-sized integers.** Both `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>` work:

```csharp
Span<nint>  signed   = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> unsigned = stackalloc nuint[4];
```

No `unsafe` context is needed since the result is assigned directly to a `Span<T>`. On this 64-bit system, each `nint`/`nuint` element is 8 bytes. This has been supported since C# 9 / .NET 5, when `nint` and `nuint` were introduced as first-class types.
